        {
            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "The inspiration for opening this issue stems from a discussion about the LuaJIT 3.0 update in the matrix room.\n\n> Mike Pall is currently adding more extensions to LuaJIT (2.1, as backports from the ongoing 3.0 work). So I don't think it makes sense to do this _now_ while aiming for a moving target.\n\nI'd emphasize that LuaJIT 3.0 plans to extend its syntax, not its built-in libraries. Without replacing it, we will never be able to make the Lua 5.1 interpreter support any new syntax. This is why I am still opening this issue now — regardless of what syntax extensions LuaJIT 3.0 adds, nvim will not support them, so LuaJIT 3.0 will have no impact on this.\n\n> It's also not at all clear which -- if any -- of these extensions are actually useful enough for Neovim (core or plugins) to take on the added maintenance and documentation burden.\n\nYou can check the features listed above; they are all minor optimizations from higher versions of Lua and maintain backward compatibility. The point of adding these features is to reduce the discrepancies between the Lua 5.1 build and the LuaJIT build, rather than to provide cool new features. To give a specific example, I once thought that `xpcall(f, msgh)` could pass arguments to `f` just like `pcall(f, ...)`, but this is feasible in LuaJIT, yet not in Lua 5.1. Subtle differences like this can lead developers to unexpectedly write code that is incompatible with Lua 5.1. Therefore, adding these features acts more as a fallback measure; they just work when you write.",
